# long64.rc with the Zend VM regenerated as ZEND_VM_KIND_TAILCALL, and nothing
# else changed. It is long64 rather than control85 so the only difference from
# the SHIPPING binary is the threading model.
#
# THE QUESTION: the interpreter currently dispatches every opcode through a
# call/return into the VM machinery. A tail-call VM chains handler to handler
# instead. Nothing in this repo has measured that on wasm.
#
# WHAT IS ALREADY VERIFIED, so the arm is worth the hours:
#
#   - wasm tail calls execute under V8 and DO eliminate stack growth: a
#     hand-assembled return_call module recursed 5,000,000 deep and returned,
#     where the same module using a plain call blew the stack at 100,000.
#   - workerd accepts them. WebAssembly.validate() answers true for a
#     return_call module in the workers lane.
#   - PHP 8.5's zend_vm_gen.php carries the backend: ZEND_VM_KIND_TAILCALL = 5,
#     with its own codegen branches and a _TAILCALL handler-name variant.
#
# THE VM GENERATES: `#define ZEND_VM_KIND ZEND_VM_KIND_TAILCALL` lands in
# Zend/zend_vm_opcodes.h and zend_vm_execute.h carries 4,895 _TAILCALL handlers.
# Verified on a real tree, which is the only place to read it from.
#
# WHAT IS NOT VERIFIED, and the first one is the likely killer:
#
# MEASURED against this emcc, before spending the hours:
#   __has_attribute(preserve_none) = 0   __has_attribute(musttail) = 1
#
# So `ZEND_OPCODE_HANDLER_CCONV`, which is `ZEND_PRESERVE_NONE` under this kind,
# expands to an UNDEFINED IDENTIFIER on wasm32 and every handler fails with
# "expected ';' after top level declarator" -- reproduced on the real macro
# shape. src/build-variant.sh passes `-DZEND_PRESERVE_NONE=` for this arm, which
# compiles clean: zend_portability.h defines that macro only behind
# HAVE_PRESERVE_NONE, which never fires here. The cost is the register-pressure
# win; musttail is untouched and is what emits return_call.
#
# HAVE_PRESERVE_NONE IS NOT DEFINED TO GET PAST THE GATE, and defining it was
# the first attempt. `-DHAVE_PRESERVE_NONE=1` makes zend_portability.h define
# ZEND_PRESERVE_NONE as `__attribute__((preserve_none))` and REDEFINE the empty
# command-line one, which is the opposite of what the flag was reaching for.
# The gate is patched to admit __wasm__ on that half instead.
#
# WHAT IS STILL NOT VERIFIED:
#
#   - Whether clang emits the tail call THROUGH THE VM's dispatch. The probe
#     above used a direct call; the VM tail-calls through a function pointer,
#     which has to become return_call_indirect. src/inspect-build.sh answers
#     this with `--expect-tailcall`: wasm-objdump over the artifact, counting
#     return_call, wired in build.yml whenever the rc sets TAIL_CALL=1. NOT a
#     benchmark -- a few percent either way proves nothing about whether the arm
#     did anything, and every other half of the build can succeed while the
#     binary stays CALL.
#   - Whether it links. No wasm target has been built with these handlers.
#
# HAVE_MUSTTAIL IS VISIBLE AT THE GATE. zend_vm_opcodes.h includes
# zend_portability.h itself, and that header is where `#if __has_attribute(
# musttail)` defines it, so there is no include-ordering hole to fall through.
#
# HOW THE KIND IS SELECTED: at COMPILE time, and NOT by regenerating the VM.
# The shipped Zend/zend_vm_execute.h already carries 4,926 _TAILCALL handlers,
# the macro definitions and 21 `#if ZEND_VM_KIND == ZEND_VM_KIND_TAILCALL`
# guards, so the header supports every kind and the C preprocessor picks one.
#
# REGENERATING IS WRONG AND WAS TRIED. `--with-vm-kind=TAILCALL` is rejected
# outright (that switch takes CALL|SWITCH|GOTO|HYBRID), and pre-defining
# ZEND_VM_GEN_KIND=5 to get around it produces a BROKEN header: the generator's
# macro-emission switch has cases for HYBRID and CALL only, so kind 5 matches
# nothing and emits no ZEND_OPCODE_HANDLER_RET, _ARGS or ZEND_VM_TAIL_CALL while
# still emitting handlers that use them. CI failed with 20 `unknown type name`
# errors on Zend/zend_execute.c.
#
# So the arm satisfies the GATE instead. On the tag php-wasm pins, php-8.5.2,
# Zend/zend_vm_opcodes.h:45 reads
#   defined(HAVE_MUSTTAIL) && defined(HAVE_PRESERVE_NONE)
#     && (defined(__x86_64__) || defined(__aarch64__))
# and measured against this emcc, HAVE_MUSTTAIL holds while the other two do
# not. build-variant.sh patches both to admit __wasm__ and supplies an empty
# -DZEND_PRESERVE_NONE=.
#
# READ THE PINNED TAG, NOT THE BRANCH. PHP-8.5 head appends
# `&& defined(__clang__)` to that condition; 8.5.2 has no such term, so a check
# written against the branch asserted something the built tree could never say.
# That is what failed run 33286797153 -- the patch had applied and the guard
# refused it. Both shapes are patched identically now.
#
# WHY THE AUTO-SELECTION NEVER PICKS IT: Zend/zend_vm_opcodes.h gates TAILCALL
# on `__x86_64__ || __aarch64__`. wasm32 is neither, so the shipped tree falls
# through to CALL. That gate is a source condition and not a capability limit,
# which is the whole premise of this arm.
#
# Score it against long64 on the AUTHENTICATED render, and report binary size,
# cold boot, warm invocation CPU and peak memory alongside the render -- a VM
# change moves all of them and a render figure alone hides a size regression.

# TAIL_CALL=1
# ZEND_LONG64=1

PHP_VERSION=8.5

MAIN_MODULE=0
ASYNCIFY=0
OPTIMIZE=2
INITIAL_MEMORY=96MB

WITH_VRZNO=1
WITH_LIBXML=static
WITH_DOM=static
WITH_SIMPLEXML=static
WITH_XML=static
WITH_YAML=static
WITH_ZLIB=static

WITH_CTYPE=1
WITH_FILTER=1
WITH_SESSION=1
WITH_TOKENIZER=1

WITH_BCMATH=0
WITH_CALENDAR=0
WITH_EXIF=0

WITH_ICONV=0
WITH_MBSTRING=0
WITH_ONIGURUMA=0
WITH_SQLITE=0
WITH_GD=0
WITH_LIBPNG=0
WITH_LIBJPEG=0
WITH_FREETYPE=0
WITH_LIBWEBP=0
WITH_LIBZIP=0
WITH_PHAR=0
WITH_XMLREADER=0
WITH_XMLWRITER=0
WITH_INTL=0
WITH_TIDY=0
WITH_SDL=0
WITH_OPENSSL=0
WITH_PDO_PGLITE=0
WITH_PDO_CFD1=0
WITH_WAITLINE=0

# opcache is compiled in; file_cache_only is set at runtime from the worker ini
CONFIGURE_FLAGS+= --enable-opcache
